From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 18:51:26 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 54/78] fix(gauntlet-tdd): append run-state notes instead of
 replacing them (gcp-dnxq)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

`gc bd update --notes` REPLACES the notes field; `--append-notes` is the
appending form. Every note write in mol-tdd-red-green used the replacing
form, which broke the one cross-step handoff the loop depends on:

  scaffold    --notes "contract_path: ..."      <- recorded
  assert-red  --notes "red_findings: ...."      <- contract_path erased
  implement   sed -n 's/^contract_path: //p'    <- empty, hard exit 1

So every run of the formula would halt at the implement gate with "no
contract_path on the root bead", after the red gate had already passed.
The failure is silent at its cause — `gc bd update` exits 0 on the write
that erased the value — and only surfaces two steps later, pointing at
the reader rather than the writer.

Switched all eight writes to --append-notes. That also stops the loop
clobbering the witness's and refinery's own annotations on a shared bead,
and the `implement` reader already takes `tail -1`, so repeated keys
resolve to the latest write.

Two regression guards in NoteWritesAppendTests, cycled red->green:
- no `bd update` line in any step may use the replacing --notes flag
  (a negative-lookbehind regex, so --append-notes does not self-match);
- if any step reads contract_path back off the notes, some step must
  append it there.

Reintroducing the bug fails both; the fix passes both.
